WEBVTT

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.409
In Department Settings, find Address and Default Map Center GPS Coordinates.

00:00:05.789 --> 00:00:09.789
Our fictional training department has a complete latitude and longitude pair.

00:00:10.149 --> 00:00:14.810
The page explains that an explicit center takes precedence over address geocoding.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.670
Read the saved values before changing them.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.459
A department map center is a viewing reference; it does not

00:00:21.459 --> 00:00:23.489
establish a unit's current position.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.829
This recording reviews the existing center without changing or saving it.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.879
Open Mapping and enter Engine in Filter marker text.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:34.110
Two named engine markers remain.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.510
The training fixture includes positions in different states, so this is a

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.109
filter demonstration rather than a local response recommendation.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.340
Filtering changes this map view.

00:00:44.709 --> 00:00:48.739
It does not rename resources, remove calls, or establish that a

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:50.799
displayed unit is ready to respond.

00:00:50.959 --> 00:00:53.870
Clear the text filter and check Hide labels.

00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:57.369
The marker symbols remain while their name labels disappear.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.619
This can reduce clutter when several objects overlap.

00:01:01.119 --> 00:01:04.529
Restore the labels while learning unfamiliar symbols, and use the

00:01:04.529 --> 00:01:08.400
category controls to decide which kinds of objects you want to inspect.

00:01:08.589 --> 00:01:12.769
Uncheck Show units and adjust the zoom to see the three training calls.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.420
The unit markers disappear from this view while the calls remain.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.589
When a resource seems missing, check the text filter and category

00:01:20.589 --> 00:01:22.790
visibility before changing its record.

00:01:23.169 --> 00:01:25.070
Restore Show units afterward.

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:29.180
Visibility in a map is separate from an object's saved location

00:01:29.209 --> 00:01:30.670
and operational status.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.530
Return to the map controls and open Manage POIs.

00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:37.660
This department initially has no point types.

00:01:38.089 --> 00:01:41.810
Add POI Type creates a category for related points.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.520
A department might organize staging locations, search assembly

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.549
points, security rally points, contractor access points, or

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:51.449
industrial response destinations.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.720
Choose a category name that its users will recognize.

00:01:54.919 --> 00:01:56.999
Name the type Academy Staging.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.289
Check Is Destination to identify this category as potential

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:03.550
destinations for a person or unit.

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:06.340
Review Color, Marker and Icon.

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:09.940
We retain the blue default color and standard pin, with

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.869
no symbol selected under Icon.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.640
A destination category describes a point's intended use; it does not

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.750
verify that a location is accessible or suitable for a real deployment.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.060
Scroll to the preview and save control.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.369
This example has no custom image, and the form says the default marker is used.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.199
The preview helps review appearance, but it is still a form.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.699
Choose Add POI Type to save the category, then read the result in the list.

00:02:37.899 --> 00:02:42.760
The list now contains Academy Staging, the saved blue color, Destination

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.820
Yes, and a point count of zero.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.850
View opens the category's information and map.

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:50.329
Add creates an individual point in it.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.369
Choose Add for this example.

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:55.870
The separate Import control is visible, but this walkthrough

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.329
creates the point manually.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:02.059
Enter the latitude and longitude in their separate fields, using decimal notation.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:06.589
Name this record Academy Staging Point, enter a clearly fictional

00:03:06.589 --> 00:03:10.499
address, and add a note identifying it as an exercise location.

00:03:10.969 --> 00:03:15.310
Review the coordinate signs and the point's purpose, then choose Add POI.

00:03:16.089 --> 00:03:20.380
For community paramedicine and mental health teams, use permitted operational

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.720
destinations and keep sensitive visit details out of general map notes.

00:03:24.909 --> 00:03:28.899
In the category's List tab, check the saved name, address,

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.269
note, latitude and longitude.

00:03:31.729 --> 00:03:33.770
The list now contains one entry.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.329
Compare the coordinate pair with what you entered.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:40.810
A successful navigation alone is not enough; this stored row gives

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.720
you the actual values to verify.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.269
Select Edit to reopen the record.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.039
The Edit POI form reopens with the saved values.

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:52.220
Check the record identity and both coordinates before

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:53.820
making any future change.

00:03:54.169 --> 00:03:56.210
This recording only reads them back.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.690
Save POI is available for a deliberate edit, but we return to

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.489
Mapping without changing the point.

00:04:02.669 --> 00:04:09.009
On the operating map, filter for Academy Staging Point and keep Show POIs checked.

00:04:09.429 --> 00:04:13.039
The actual saved marker and its name are now visible on the map.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.190
Compare its position with recognizable surroundings.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:20.049
A saved coordinate can be plotted accurately and still describe the

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:24.709
wrong intended location, so review the placement before operational use.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.070
Open the marker to inspect its popup.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.530
Check the point name and its Academy Staging category.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.069
Use the saved List or Edit view when you need to review the

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:36.289
complete note or exact coordinates.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.800
This connection between a named record and its actual map position is useful

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.310
for search teams, security departments, wildland contractors and industrial

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.740
teams as well as fire and EMS.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:52.550
Uncheck Show POIs while retaining the point's name in the text filter.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:54.149
The marker disappears.

00:04:54.539 --> 00:04:56.600
This does not delete the saved point.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.079
If a dispatcher cannot find a known location, this is one concrete

00:05:01.079 --> 00:05:04.350
visibility setting to check before creating a duplicate record.

00:05:04.559 --> 00:05:06.260
Check Show POIs again.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.679
The same saved marker returns without recreating or editing it.

00:05:11.049 --> 00:05:14.429
Clear the text filter when you want the broader operating picture.

00:05:14.809 --> 00:05:19.410
Keep a responsible owner for reviewing destination names, notes and coordinates

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.369
as department operations change.

00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:26.409
Open Map Icon Legend and find Stations, hydrants and points of interest.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.300
Academy Staging now appears in that group.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:34.290
The legend explicitly states that these icons do not change with status.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:38.780
Keep this distinction clear: a staging point is a place, while

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:42.419
a responding unit or member has a separate operational status.

00:05:42.829 --> 00:05:46.800
A location marker alone does not tell you who is available to use it.

